# Choosing a design, and turning an estimate into a policy

## One Estimate, Three Decisions
voice: male
level: advanced

    text t_year "Oregon, 2008" x 500 y 700 font_size 30 hidden true
    text t_apply "≈10,000 Medicaid slots — ≈90,000 adults sign up" x 500 y 650 font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_lottery "≈30,000 names drawn by lottery  →  Z_i = 1" x 500 y 605 font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_takeup "only ≈1 in 4 of them actually enrolls  →  D_i = 1" x 500 y 560 font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_est "LATE:  −9.2 points on the rate of depression" #b5482e x 500 y 480 font_size 32 hidden true
    text t_est_sub "for compliers · in Oregon · in 2008" #8f887c x 500 y 440 font_size 24 hidden true
    box stage_design "1. Design" hidden true
    box stage_transport "2. Transport" hidden true
    box stage_price "3. Price" hidden true
    edge e1 stage_design -> stage_transport hidden true
    edge e2 stage_transport -> stage_price hidden true
    label label_design "Which assumption do you sign?" below attach_to stage_design hidden true
    label label_transport "Do the compliers travel?" below #8a5fa8 attach_to stage_transport hidden true
    label label_price "$ per QALY" below #8a5fa8 attach_to stage_price hidden true
    label label_e1 "…but for whom?" above attach_to e1 hidden true
    label label_e2 "…and worth what?" above attach_to e2 hidden true
    mark mark_transport target stage_transport kind circle hidden true

This series follows one causal estimate from a lottery in Oregon to a line in a health budget — because the step that usually decides policy is not the regression, but what comes after it.
    draw t_year

In 2008 Oregon had money to cover about ten thousand adults, and about ninety thousand signed up.
    draw t_apply

So the state drew names — roughly thirty thousand of them — and that draw is our instrument, Z_i.
    draw t_lottery

Only about a quarter of those drawn ended up enrolled, so winning the lottery is very far from being covered, D_i.
    draw t_takeup

    pause 0.4

Scale the intention-to-treat up by that take-up and you get the headline: coverage cut the rate of depression by about nine percentage points.
    draw t_est t_est_sub

The small print carries the whole lecture — compliers, Oregon, 2008.
    highlight t_est_sub effect glow

    wait

An econometrics course stops at that number; a budget office is still three arguments away from spending on it.
    clear keep ["t_est","t_est_sub"]

Park it at the top of the board and watch what must happen to it on the way down.
    move t_est t_est_sub by [0,190] duration 1

First, the design — a lottery, an instrument, a difference-in-differences, a discontinuity, a synthetic control — each one asking you to sign a different assumption.
    draw stage_design label_design

    pause 0.3

Second, transport: whether an effect measured on Oregon's compliers says anything about the people another state would actually cover.
    draw e1 label_e1 stage_transport label_transport

    pause 0.3

Third, price: turning an effect on depression into a cost per quality-adjusted life year.
    draw e2 label_e2 stage_price label_price

    wait

Design asks for the assumption that makes coverage as good as randomly assigned — exclusion and monotonicity, or parallel trends, or continuity at the cutoff.
    text sign_1 "Design: the assumption that makes D_i as good as random" #2f6b8f x 500 y 225 font_size 24

Oregon buys that almost for free, which is why every observational design in this course was judged against it.
    highlight stage_design effect glow

Transport asks for something no Oregon data can settle: that Oregon's compliers resemble the people your policy would enrol.
    text sign_2 "Transport: Oregon's compliers stand in for your population" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 180 font_size 24

And price asks for two more things no regression supplies — what a year lived with depression is worth against full health, and the threshold above which the money is judged not worth spending.
    text sign_3 "Price: a weight per year of health, and a threshold in dollars" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 135 font_size 24

England's NICE works to roughly twenty to thirty thousand pounds per QALY — a number set by judgement, and openly defended as judgement.
    point at.ref sign_3 gesture underline delivery soft

When Oregon's two-year results landed, both sides of the Medicaid expansion fight quoted the same estimates and disagreed entirely about the two steps below.
    highlight t_est effect pulse

    wait

    ask "Click the step where a flawless Oregon estimate can still be the wrong number for another state." intro "Your turn." answer stage_transport widget click
        right: Transport — the estimate belongs to Oregon's compliers, and another state covers different people.

The weakest link is rarely the econometrics; it is the argument no dataset can adjudicate.
    draw mark_transport delivery grave

    quiz "In a cost per QALY figure, which input is a value judgement wearing a parameter's clothes?" intro "One check before we go." required true
        right: The weight — what a year with depression is worth against full health comes from a valuation survey, not from any causal design.
        wrong: Both the effect and the cost can be estimated; what a year of health is worth cannot be.
        * The effect of coverage on depression
        + The weight on a year lived with depression
        * The cost of enrolling one adult

Nine percentage points, for compliers, in Oregon, in 2008: identification was the easy part, and the next four parts are the three arguments that follow it.

## Picking the Design You Can Defend
voice: male
level: advanced

Now that we have seen one estimate pull three different decisions, the harder question is which design earns the right to produce that number a budget office will actually spend money on.
    text title "Design follows data — and the assumption you must defend" x 500 y 706 font_size 29

Three columns: the data you actually have, the design that data permits, and the one assumption you agree to defend in public.
    path hline #8f887c points [[40,634],[960,634]]
    text h_data "Data you actually have" x 165 y 660 font_size 23
    text h_design "Design" x 470 y 660 font_size 23
    text h_assume "What you agree to defend" #8a5fa8 x 790 y 660 font_size 23
    parallel true

    pause 0.4

Oregon, 2008: money for about ten thousand Medicaid slots, ninety thousand adults signed up, and the state drew roughly thirty thousand names by lottery.
    text data_rct "A randomized slot" x 165 y 572 font_size 21

A randomly assigned slot buys you a randomized trial, whose honest headline is the intention-to-treat — drawn versus not drawn, whatever people did afterwards.
    text design_rct "RCT / ITT" #2f6b8f x 470 y 572 font_size 21

The bill here is small: the draw really was random, and both groups were followed up the same way — which is why this row is the benchmark every other row is judged against.
    text assume_rct "The draw is truly random" #8a5fa8 x 790 y 572 font_size 21

    pause 0.5

    wait

But randomizing the offer is not randomizing coverage: only about a quarter of the people drawn ended up enrolled.
    text data_iv "Random slot, 25% take-up" x 165 y 487 font_size 21

So winning the lottery becomes an instrument for coverage D i, and dividing the intention-to-treat by that first stage of one quarter scales the number up roughly fourfold.
    text design_iv "IV → LATE" #2f6b8f x 470 y 487 font_size 21

Which means you now defend two further claims: the lottery moved health only by changing coverage, and losing it never pushed anyone into Medicaid.
    text assume_iv "Exclusion + monotonicity" #8a5fa8 x 790 y 487 font_size 21

    quiz "Losing the lottery makes people seek free clinic care they'd otherwise skip. Which assumption breaks?" intro "A quick check."
        right: Exclusion breaks — the lottery is now moving health through a second channel, not only through coverage.
        wrong: Monotonicity is about who enrols, and randomization is untouched — the problem is a second channel.
        + Exclusion restriction
        * Monotonicity
        * Random assignment

    wait

Most questions arrive with no lottery at all; what you have is a date — in 2014 some states expanded Medicaid under the ACA, and others refused.
    text data_did "A policy date + controls" x 165 y 402 font_size 21
    text design_did "DiD" #b5482e x 470 y 402 font_size 21

Difference-in-differences buys its counterfactual from the non-expanding states, and the price is parallel trends: a claim about a path that never happened, which pre-trends can only rhyme with.
    text assume_did "Parallel trends" #8a5fa8 x 790 y 402 font_size 21

And when states expand in different years, the naive two-way fixed effects estimate quietly starts comparing late adopters with already-treated early ones — the staggered trouble from lecture twelve.
    highlight assume_did effect glow

    pause 0.4

Sometimes the assignment rule is simply a number: in the United States, coverage changes sharply on the sixty-fifth birthday.
    text data_rd "A sharp cutoff (age 65)" x 165 y 317 font_size 21
    text design_rd "RD" #b5482e x 470 y 317 font_size 21

Regression discontinuity compares people just under and just over that line, and you defend that nothing else jumps exactly there — which is why the covariate and density checks are the argument, not a formality.
    text assume_rd "Nothing else jumps at 65" #8a5fa8 x 790 y 317 font_size 21

    pause 0.4

And sometimes there is exactly one treated unit — one state, one reform — and no comparison group anywhere.
    text data_sc "One treated unit + donors" x 165 y 232 font_size 21
    text design_sc "Synthetic control" #b5482e x 470 y 232 font_size 21

Synthetic control weights donor states into a blend that tracks the treated one before the policy — as Abadie's California tobacco study did — and you defend that the blend would have kept tracking it after.
    text assume_sc "Pre-period fit keeps holding" #8a5fa8 x 790 y 232 font_size 21

    wait

    ask "Only one state changed its policy, and dozens did not. Click the design you would have to defend." intro "One for you." answer design_sc widget click
        right: Synthetic control — one treated unit, a donor pool, and a pre-period fit that you promise would have held.

Read straight down this column and the choice stops looking technical.
    focus assume_rct assume_iv assume_did assume_rd assume_sc

Every row sells the same product — a causal number — and differs only in what you have promised to defend.
    mark ann_col target h_assume kind box

And if the assumption in the row you chose fails, nothing warns you — the failure travels straight into the price of the expansion.
    highlight design_did design_rd design_sc effect glow

    quiz "Which of these can you actually test in the data?" required true
        right: The first stage is a fact you can measure — about a quarter of those drawn enrolled; the other two are claims about a world you never observe.
        wrong: Parallel trends and continuity at 65 concern counterfactuals — you can stress-test them, but never observe them.
        * Parallel trends after treatment
        + The lottery's first stage
        * Continuity of potential outcomes at 65

So the rule is this: not the design that sounds strongest, but the assumption you can defend to the people who will spend money on your estimate.
    text note "You don't pick the strongest design — you pick the assumption you can defend." x 500 y 124 font_size 22
    mark ann_note target note kind box
    delivery grave

Oregon handed us a defensible assumption for free; every design below it is an argument for why our data almost did.

## Whose effect is it? Compliers in Oregon
voice: male
level: advanced
use: icon_array
with:
    total: 100
    groups:
      - {label: Compliers (enrolled), count: 25, color: supply}
    rest_label: Never-takers
    title: 100 names drawn in the Oregon lottery

Now that we've seen how to pick a design you can defend, take the one we trust most — Oregon's 2008 lottery — and ask the question a budget office will ask: whose effect is this number, before you price an expansion with it?
    draw title

Of every hundred names drawn from that list, only about twenty-five ended up enrolled in Medicaid.
    draw group_0 legend_0

    pause 0.4

The other seventy-five won a lottery that changed nothing at all about their coverage.
    draw rest legend_rest

Winning only bought the right to apply — return the form, document your income, enrol — and roughly half never sent the paperwork back.
    focus rest

    wait

Under monotonicity these twenty-five are the compliers: the people whose coverage the draw actually moved, plus a handful of always-takers who would have found coverage anyway.
    highlight group_0 effect glow

The seventy-five are never-takers — uninsured whether their name came out or not, so they carry no information about what insurance does.
    focus rest

    ask "Click the people whose effect the LATE describes." intro "Quick check —" answer group_0 widget click
        right: The LATE belongs to the compliers, the quarter whose enrollment the draw changed.
        wrong: The never-takers never changed coverage, so they contribute nothing to the estimate.

We can count the compliers exactly — a quarter of the draw — but we can never point at any single one, because nobody shows us what they would have done had the draw gone the other way.
    point at.ref group_0 gesture circle

    wait

So the winners-versus-losers comparison is diluted by three quarters of people it never touched.
    clear

    text t_head "Rescaling the lottery estimate" x 500 y 660 font_size 34

The raw comparison, the intention-to-treat, is roughly a hundred and ninety-five dollars more annual medical spending for a name drawn than a name not drawn.
    text t_itt "ITT (drawn vs not drawn) ≈ $195 per year" x 500 y 545 font_size 30

The first stage is that quarter: winning raised the chance of coverage by about twenty-five percentage points, once you subtract the few who were covered without winning.
    text t_fs "First stage (coverage gain) = 0.25" x 500 y 450 font_size 30

Divide one by the other, and the effect on compliers is four times as large — about seven hundred and eighty dollars a year.
    text t_late "LATE = ITT ÷ 0.25 ≈ $780 per year, for compliers" #b5482e x 500 y 345 font_size 30

That is the published Oregon spending effect, and it was never an effect on everybody.
    mark box_late target t_late kind box
    delivery grave

    quiz "The ITT is about $195 a year and a quarter of those drawn enrolled. What is the LATE?"
        intro: Let's check the arithmetic.
        right: You divide by the first stage of 0.25, which multiplies the ITT by four — about $780 a year.
        wrong: Dividing by a first stage below one always makes the estimate bigger, never smaller.
        * About $49
        * About $195
        + About $780

The same division multiplies the noise: rescaling by a quarter makes the confidence interval four times wider, which is exactly why Oregon's health results were so hard to call.
    text t_se "Dividing by 0.25 multiplies the standard error by 4 too" #8f887c x 500 y 230 font_size 24

    wait

Keep that ratio in mind, and go back to the people.
    clear

Here they are again — and now the number has an owner.
    show title group_0 rest legend_0 legend_rest

Seven hundred and eighty dollars is the effect for these twenty-five, who are a quarter of the ninety thousand who signed up, who were themselves the Oregonians who heard about the list and put their names down.
    focus group_0

Lift that number off the shelf to price an expansion elsewhere, and you have quietly assumed your newly covered population behaves like the sliver of Oregonians a lottery could move.
    delivery grave

    quiz "Whose effect is the Oregon LATE?" intro "One more."
        right: It is the effect on compliers — the quarter whose coverage the draw actually moved.
        wrong: The seventy-five never-takers never changed coverage, so they contribute nothing to the estimate.
        * Every uninsured adult in Oregon
        * Everyone who entered the lottery
        + Those whose enrollment the lottery changed

So the Oregon estimate is not the effect of insurance; it's the effect of insurance on the one in four whose enrollment a lottery could change — countable, unnameable, and the population we have to argue about next time.
    highlight group_0 effect glow

## Cost per QALY: Causal or Value Judgement?
voice: male
level: advanced

    text title "Cost per QALY" x 500 y 712 font_size 30 hidden true
    text eq_lhs "ICER =" x 215 y 630 font_size 30 hidden true
    text eq_num "Δ Cost" x 420 y 662 font_size 28 hidden true
    path eq_bar points [[320,634],[520,634]] hidden true
    text eq_den "Δ QALYs" x 420 y 602 font_size 28 hidden true
    text eq_rhs "= dollars per QALY gained" x 700 y 630 font_size 24 hidden true
    path rule_top #8f887c points [[60,560],[940,560]] hidden true
    path divider dashed #8f887c points [[500,548],[500,232]] hidden true
    text head_left "Estimated (causal)" #2f6b8f x 270 y 522 font_size 25 hidden true
    text head_right "Chosen (value judgement)" #b5482e x 740 y 522 font_size 25 hidden true
    text c1 "Δ spending: +$778 / year" #2f6b8f x 270 y 468 font_size 21 hidden true
    text c2 "Δ depression: −30%" #2f6b8f x 270 y 404 font_size 21 hidden true
    text c3 "Δ blood sugar: ≈ 0" #2f6b8f x 270 y 340 font_size 21 hidden true
    text c4 "Compliance: 25% take-up" #2f6b8f x 270 y 276 font_size 21 hidden true
    text v1 "Utility weight for depression" #b5482e x 740 y 468 font_size 21 hidden true
    text v2 "Discount rate: 3% or 1.5%?" #b5482e x 740 y 404 font_size 21 hidden true
    text v3 "Perspective: payer or society?" #b5482e x 740 y 340 font_size 21 hidden true
    text v4 "Threshold: $50,000 per QALY?" #b5482e x 740 y 276 font_size 21 hidden true

Now that we have seen what it takes to carry Oregon's complier effect to another state, one step remains — the one where a committee decides whether to cover the thing at all.
    draw title

The incremental cost-effectiveness ratio: the extra cost of covering someone, divided by the extra quality-adjusted life years it buys.
    draw eq_lhs eq_num eq_bar eq_den

Out comes one number in dollars per QALY, and NICE in England, the Nordic agencies and American payers all price coverage from it.
    draw eq_rhs

Only half of that number was ever estimated — and the chosen half moves it further than any confidence interval does.
    highlight eq_rhs effect glow delivery grave

    wait

So split the formula down the middle — on the left, everything a research design can actually deliver.
    draw rule_top divider head_left

From the Oregon lottery: enrolling in Medicaid raised annual medical spending by about seven hundred and seventy-eight dollars.
    draw c1

On the benefit side, the sharpest finding — coverage cut the rate of depression by roughly thirty percent.
    draw c2

And the celebrated null: blood pressure, cholesterol and blood sugar barely moved, estimated tightly enough to rule out large effects, not small ones.
    draw c3

All of it scaled by the quarter of those drawn who actually enrolled, so these are complier effects, not effects on everyone.
    draw c4

Every line here costs an assumption you agreed to defend — random assignment, exclusion, and the leap from compliers to the population you care about.
    focus head_left c1 c2 c3 c4

    wait

Now the other side: to turn less depression into QALYs, somebody has to say what a year without depression is worth.
    draw head_right v1

That weight comes from a survey — typically EQ-5D valuations, where members of the public trade years of life against health states. No design identifies it.
    arrow arrow_weight c2 -> v1 curved #8a5fa8
    label arrow_label "worth how much?" above #8a5fa8 attach_to arrow_weight

Say that weight is three hundredths of a QALY per year: seven seventy-eight divided by 0.03 is about twenty-six thousand dollars per QALY.
    text res1 "$778 ÷ 0.03 = $26,000 per QALY" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 175 font_size 26

    wait

Set the weight to 0.02 and the same causal estimate costs thirty-nine thousand; set it to 0.05 and it costs sixteen thousand.
    text res2 "0.02 → $39,000      0.05 → $16,000" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 122 font_size 22
    mark mark_range target res2 kind box
    delivery grave

Nothing about Oregon changed — the estimate never moved, only the price we put on feeling well.
    highlight c2 effect glow

Then the discount rate: benefits arriving in 2040 count for less than benefits today, and three percent versus one and a half is a stance on the future, not a measurement of it.
    draw v2

Perspective decides whose dollars count — Medicaid's budget alone, or also the catastrophic out-of-pocket bills Oregon nearly eliminated, and the work that illness costs.
    draw v3

And the threshold that turns a ratio into a yes or no; the American fifty-thousand figure is usually traced back to what dialysis cost Medicare in the seventies, not to any estimate of what health is worth.
    draw v4

NICE has used twenty to thirty thousand pounds since 1999, while Claxton's team estimated that the health displaced elsewhere in the NHS costs about thirteen thousand — if that is right, approvals at the official threshold destroy more health than they create.
    highlight v4 effect pulse

    wait

    ask "Click the parameter that decides whether $26,000 per QALY counts as good value." answer v4 widget click
        intro: One quick check on the figure.
        right: The threshold does that work — the ratio itself never says yes or no.
        wrong: The ratio is only ever compared with something: the threshold converts it into a decision.

    quiz "Which change moves the cost per QALY with no new data at all?" intro "And two on the logic."
        right: The discount rate is chosen, so changing it moves the number without a single new observation.
        wrong: Sample size and first-stage strength change the estimate's precision; they need data.
        * A larger Oregon sample
        + A lower discount rate
        * A stronger first stage

    quiz "The $778 spending effect and the EQ-5D utility weight differ in kind. How?"
        right: The spending effect is identified by the lottery; the weight is elicited from people's stated preferences.
        wrong: Only one of them has a research design behind it — the weight comes from a valuation survey.
        * Both are causal estimates
        + One is estimated, one is elicited preference
        * Both are value judgements

That's {score} out of {score_total}.
    delivery brisk

So report the ratio as what it is: an estimate defended by one assumption, carried to a new population by another, and then multiplied by four numbers nobody estimated.
    text closer "Half estimated, half chosen — report both." x 500 y 58 font_size 24

Across this series the road from design to decision was one chain of things you agree to defend — and you can now see where the chain changes character: at the end, honesty means naming a choice, not an identification strategy.
    delivery grave

